.TH PARTRESIZE 8 "2026" "partresize 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
partresize \- safety-first Linux partition and filesystem resizing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B partresize
.I command
.RI [ target ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B partresize
resizes partitions and the filesystems inside them, choosing the safest
workflow automatically. It discovers the full storage stack (partition table,
LUKS, LVM, filesystem), keeps a conservative capability matrix, and never
reorders steps in a way that can corrupt data. Operations that cannot be done
safely on a mounted filesystem are performed offline (unmount/resize/remount)
or scheduled for a one-time reboot-assisted maintenance pass built from the
system's own initramfs \(em no external media required.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
List block devices.
.TP
.B inspect \fItarget\fR
Show the resolved storage topology and current sizes.
.TP
.B capabilities \fItarget\fR
Show whether the filesystem can grow/shrink online/offline.
.TP
.B plan \fItarget\fR \fIsize-option\fR
Show the ordered operation plan. Never changes anything.
.TP
.B resize \fItarget\fR \fIsize-option\fR
Resize the filesystem/partition of \fItarget\fR.
.TP
.B grow \fItarget\fR [\fB--by\fR \fIS\fR | \fB--size\fR \fIS\fR]
Grow to fill free space, or by/to a size.
.TP
.B shrink \fItarget\fR \fB--size\fR \fIS\fR | \fB--by\fR \fIS\fR
Shrink to/by a size (offline as required).
.TP
.B backup-table \fIdisk\fR [\fIfile\fR]
Write a restorable dump of the partition table.
.TP
.B restore-table \fIdisk\fR \fIfile\fR
Restore a partition table from a dump.
.TP
.B status \fR|\fB cancel \fR|\fB recover
Show, cancel, or recover an in-progress/scheduled operation.
.SH SIZE OPTIONS
.TP
.B --size \fIS\fR
Absolute target size.
.TP
.B --grow \fIS\fR
Grow by \fIS\fR (omit \fIS\fR with \fBgrow\fR to fill free space).
.TP
.B --shrink \fIS\fR
Shrink by \fIS\fR.
.TP
.B --by \fIS\fR
Amount for the \fBgrow\fR/\fBshrink\fR subcommands.
.PP
Sizes accept binary (KiB, MiB, GiB, TiB) and decimal (KB, MB, GB, TB) units;
a bare K/M/G/T is binary. Examples: 300G, 80GiB, 1000MB.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --dry-run
Print exactly what would happen; change nothing.
.TP
.B --offline
Perform an offline resize now (non-root: unmount, resize, remount).
.TP
.B --schedule
Prepare an offline resize for the next reboot (works for the root filesystem).
.TP
.B -y, --yes
Do not prompt for confirmation.
.TP
.B -v, --verbose
Print each external command as it runs.
.SH TARGET
A mount point (\fI/home\fR, \fI/\fR) or a block device
(\fI/dev/nvme0n1p3\fR, \fI/dev/mapper/vg-home\fR). The tool resolves the target
to its filesystem's backing device; it will not resize a whole disk given in
place of a partition.
.SH SAFETY
Destructive operations require root and an explicit confirmation (or
.BR --yes ).
The filesystem is always shrunk before the partition; a partition is never made
smaller than the filesystem it holds; and
.I filesystem_size \(<= block_device_size
is enforced throughout. Partition-table and LVM-metadata backups are taken
before destructive shrinks. See
.B docs/safety.md\fR.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PARTRESIZE_STATE_DIR
Directory for operation state and backups (default
.IR /var/lib/partresize ).
.TP
.B PARTRESIZE_TEST_MODE
When set to 1, refuse any device that is not a loop/dm test device.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Error (see message).
.TP
.B 2
Plan is infeasible / operation refused.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/partresize/state.json
Persistent operation state for recovery.
.TP
.I /var/lib/partresize/backups/<op-id>/
Partition-table and LVM-metadata backups.
.SH CAVEATS
Partition resizing is inherently risky.
.B partresize
cannot guarantee recovery from hardware failure or unexpected power loss.
Back up important data first.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lsblk (8),
.BR sfdisk (8),
.BR resize2fs (8),
.BR xfs_growfs (8),
.BR btrfs (8),
.BR lvreduce (8),
.BR lvextend (8),
.BR cryptsetup (8)
